   "text": "Now, someone claps at the far end of the kitchen. Sound is air pushing on air. Without air, there would be nothing to push, and no sound at all. In a room, it moves about three hundred forty meters a second. That's why you never see it travel. The room is five meters across, so the clap crosses it in about fifteen thousandths of a second. Here, that's fifteen seconds. There it goes: a ring of squeezed air, crossing the room. Imagine standing at the far wall. You'd hear the clap only when this ring reached you. It reaches the wall, and bounces back. That bounce is an echo. You use this trick in every storm. Sound covers about a kilometer in three seconds. So count the seconds between a flash and its thunder, and divide by three. That's roughly how many kilometers away the lightning is. Real quick: like and follow. It genuinely helps. Now, where were we? But a clap is slow. Something on this table is much faster than sound, and it's about to go through an apple.",
